Both Web Development and Data Science are solid options for 2026. Which is "better" depends on your interests, how you like to work, and how quickly you want to start earning or building a portfolio.

Key factors to consider
- Interest and daily work
  - Web Development: you’ll build features, interfaces, and apps end-to-end. If you enjoy shipping products, UI/UX, and problem-solving in code, this fits you.
  - Data Science: you’ll explore data, build models, and turn insights into decisions. If you enjoy math, statistics, data storytelling, and experimentation, this suits you.
- Market signals
  - Web development: steady demand, opportunities across startups, agencies, and big tech. Evergreen skillset; high demand for front-end (React/Vue), back-end (Node, Python, Java), and full-stack positions.
  - Data Science: strong demand, especially with AI/ML adoption, data engineering, and MLOps roles growing. Often requires deeper technical foundation (statistics, modeling, data pipelines) and ongoing learning.
- Time to value and entry paths
  - Web Development: typically faster to land an entry-level role if you build a solid portfolio (3–6 months of focused learning and projects).
  - Data Science: can take longer to prove value (6–12+ months of projects, coursework, and a portfolio with end-to-end data work).
- Salary and advancement
  - Data Science roles can start higher in some markets, but compete with rigorous expectations. Senior data roles (ML/AI engineer, data engineer, MLOps) are high-paying but require deeper specialization.
  - Web Development offers strong earning potential, especially with full-stack proficiency, performance optimization, and cloud/native devops skills. Senior salaries vary by region and company size.
- Future-proofing
  - AI and automation will impact both: web apps increasingly rely on AI features, and data roles expand into data engineering, ML, and MLOps. A hybrid skill set (e.g., web dev with data visualization or ML-integration) can be especially valuable.

A practical plan to decide
1) Self-check: Are you more excited by building user-facing apps or by analyzing data and telling stories with numbers?
2) Try a short pilot: spend 6 weeks on a small web-dev project (portfolio-worthy) and 6 weeks on a data-science project (end-to-end: data cleaning, simple model, visualization). See which you prefer.
3) Consider a hybrid track: you can start as a web developer and add data visualization, analytics, or ML features to your stack (e.g., dashboards, product analytics, or AI-powered web components).
4) Set a 6–12 month roadmap:
   - If Web Development: deepen front-end (React/Vue), learn a back-end (Node.js, Python/Flask or Django), databases, APIs, and deployment. Build 3–4 polished projects.
   - If Data Science: solidify Python, Pandas, SQL, statistics; learn ML basics (scikit-learn), data visualization, data pipelines basics; complete 2–3 end-to-end projects with clean storytelling and deployment (e.g., a BI dashboard or a simple deployed model).
5) Portfolio and job-market plan: tailor projects to roles you want (full-stack for web dev; data analytics/ML for data science). Network, apply to a mix of startups, mid-sized companies, and remote roles.

